Autism Spectrum Australia (Aspect) has launched an educational web game featuring characters from **Thomas & Friends**, developed to help autistic children practise social and communication skills in an engaging, familiar setting.

The game uses short, structured activities built around Thomas and the other engines. Children are prompted to:

- recognise facial expressions and emotions;
- interpret social cues and everyday situations;
- take turns, listen and follow instructions;
- make choices and anticipate what may happen next; and
- practise language used in common social interactions.

The Thomas & Friends setting is intended to increase motivation and reduce uncertainty. Many autistic children have a strong interest in trains or the series, while the game’s predictable routines, clear visual prompts and immediate feedback make social information easier to process. Because it is web-based, children can repeat activities at their own pace and use the game with parents, teachers or therapists, who can pause and discuss what characters are feeling or how a similar situation might be handled in real life.

Aspect presented the game as a supplementary learning resource rather than a stand-alone therapy. Its main value lies in providing a low-pressure environment in which children can rehearse skills before applying them at home, at school and in the community. Individual responses will vary, so adult guidance and connection to real-world practice remain important.
